 (279,344,'L1 packing and penalties shape the first-match comparison','The narrator discusses horizontal pyramid packing and the choice to trust two strong scorers while another robot defends. He also attributes an important score change to late defensive penalties.','Mechanical presentation at L1 affects packing density and time per placement. Defensive output should be measured net of penalties, not solely by reduced opposing cycles. Verify the official score before assigning the result to any one foul.'),
 (837,902,'A close displayed score can conceal unequal remaining work','The commentary interprets missed cycles and pressure while comparing the alliances’ remaining coral opportunities.','Use remaining pieces, available nodes and expected completion time to assess a deficit. Do not treat inferred rushed-driver psychology as a team statement. A scorer’s rare misses may have multiple causes, including piece orientation, defense or mechanical condition.'),
 (1396,1461,'A defensive counter can be constrained by nearby scorers','In the later match, the narrator considers why a defender may avoid entering a station approach when nearby opponents can exploit protected geometry, and contrasts several placements/role choices.','Series adaptation should retain the cost of the attempted counter. “Just get in front” is insufficient when every available entry risks a foul or abandons another threat. Keep match-one, match-two and match-three roles distinct.')
